        contentUrl: null,
        content: `WORKING AT HEIGHTS — LEGISLATION & THE HIERARCHY OF CONTROLS (RIIWHS204E)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: THE LEGAL DEFINITION OF "WORKING AT HEIGHTS"
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Falls from height remain one of the leading causes of death and permanent disability in the Australian construction and mining industries. The legal framework surrounding working at heights is consequently strict, highly prescriptive, and rigorously enforced.

What constitutes "Working at Heights"?
A common and dangerous misconception is that height safety regulations only apply above a certain measurement (e.g., 2 metres). Under the harmonised WHS Regulations, there is NO minimum height threshold for fall hazards.

A fall hazard exists if a person is working in a situation where they could fall from one level to another, and that fall is reasonably likely to cause injury. This includes:
→ Falling from an elevated structure (scaffold, roof, ladder, EWP).
→ Falling into a depth (an excavation, trench, pit, or shaft).
→ Falling through a fragile surface (skylights, asbestos cement roofing, brittle plastic sheeting).
→ Falling off the back of a flatbed truck or trailer.

While the general duty to manage fall risks applies at any height, specific High-Risk Construction Work (HRCW) regulations—which mandate the creation of a Safe Work Method Statement (SWMS)—are triggered when there is a risk of falling more than 2 metres. However, serious injuries, including fatal head injuries, frequently occur from falls of less than 1 metre.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: THE HIERARCHY OF FALL CONTROLS — A LEGAL MANDATE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When a fall hazard is identified, the PCBU (Person Conducting a Business or Undertaking) and the workers involved cannot simply choose their preferred safety equipment. The WHS Regulations mandate a specific Hierarchy of Fall Controls. You must apply the highest level of control that is reasonably practicable before considering lower levels.

LEVEL 1: ELIMINATION (Do the work on the ground)
The only way to completely eliminate a fall risk is to perform the work on the ground or from a solid construction.
→ Example: Assembling a roof structure or communications tower on the ground and lifting the completed assembly into place with a crane.
→ Example: Lowering a light pole to the ground using a hinge mechanism to change the bulb, rather than climbing it.
→ Solid Construction: Working from an area that has a structurally sound floor, edge protection all around, safe access and egress, and no fragile roof materials.

LEVEL 2: PASSIVE FALL PREVENTION (Isolate the hazard)
If the work must be done at height, you must provide a passive fall prevention device. "Passive" means that once installed, the worker does not need to do anything to be protected (they don't need to wear a harness or clip in).
→ Edge Protection: Temporary guardrail systems, scaffold handrails, or physical barriers. A compliant guardrail must have a top rail (900-1100mm high), a mid-rail, and a toeboard (to prevent tools kicking off).
→ Elevating Work Platforms (EWPs): Scissor lifts and boom lifts provide a passive barrier (the basket) while working.
→ Fall Protection Covers: Heavy-duty, secured covers placed over penetrations, voids, or skylights.

LEVEL 3: WORK POSITIONING SYSTEMS
If passive protection is not reasonably practicable, use a work positioning system. This is a harness-based system where the lanyard length is physically adjusted so that the worker is in tension and CANNOT reach the unprotected edge.
→ Travel Restraint System: The worker is connected to an anchor point with a fixed-length lanyard that is physically too short to allow them to step off the edge.
→ A travel restraint system prevents the fall from occurring at all.

LEVEL 4: FALL ARREST SYSTEMS (The Last Resort)
If none of the above are reasonably practicable, a fall arrest system is used. This system DOES NOT prevent the fall. It is designed to catch the worker after they have fallen, stopping them before they hit the ground, and limiting the impact forces on their body.
→ This is the least preferred option because the worker still experiences a fall, is subjected to violent arrest forces, and is left suspended in mid-air, requiring an immediate, complex emergency rescue to prevent suspension trauma.

LEVEL 5: LADDERS AND ADMINISTRATIVE CONTROLS
Ladders should only be used for access/egress or for short-duration, light-duty work where three points of contact can be maintained. Administrative controls (like "no-go" zones marked with tape) are the weakest form of protection and should never be relied upon alone to prevent a fall.`
