        content: `ACCESS EQUIPMENT — SCAFFOLDING, EWPS, LADDERS & DROPPED OBJECTS

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: SCAFFOLDING SAFETY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Scaffolding provides a highly effective Level 2 passive fall prevention environment, but it introduces its own significant risks during erection, alteration, and use.

The Scaffolding Tag System:
You must never access a scaffold without verifying its status via the "ScaffTag" (or equivalent) positioned at the access point.
→ Green Tag: "Safe for Use." The scaffold has been inspected by a competent person and is fully compliant. It specifies the duty rating (Light, Medium, or Heavy).
→ Yellow Tag: "Caution - Modification in Progress" or restricted use. Specific conditions apply.
→ Red Tag (or NO TAG): "UNSAFE - DO NOT USE." The scaffold is incomplete, damaged, being erected, or being dismantled. Access is strictly prohibited to everyone except licensed scaffolders.

Scaffold User Rules:
→ Never alter a scaffold: Only a worker holding a High-Risk Work Licence (HRWL) in Scaffolding (Basic, Intermediate, or Advanced) is legally permitted to erect, alter, or dismantle scaffolding. If a handrail is in your way, you cannot remove it. If a plank is missing, you cannot just lay down a piece of timber. You must contact a scaffolder.
→ Respect Load Ratings:
   - Light Duty (225 kg per bay): Suitable for painters, electricians, and carpenters. No heavy material storage.
   - Medium Duty (450 kg per bay): Suitable for general construction, blockwork, and plastering.
   - Heavy Duty (675 kg per bay): Required for heavy masonry, demolition, or large material storage.
→ Keep decks clear: Do not store materials on access walkways. Maintain a minimum clear width of 450mm at all times.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: ELEVATING WORK PLATFORMS (EWPs)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

EWPs include Scissor Lifts, Telescopic Boom Lifts, and Articulated Boom Lifts (Cherry Pickers).

Licensing and Competency:
→ Boom Lifts with a capacity ≥ 11 metres: Require a High-Risk Work Licence (Class WP).
→ Scissor Lifts & Boom Lifts < 11 metres: Require proof of training (e.g., an EWPA Yellow Card) and a site-specific Verification of Competency (VOC).

The Harness Rule in EWPs:
→ Boom Lifts: You MUST wear a full-body fall arrest harness with a short lanyard attached to the engineered anchor point inside the basket at all times. This is because boom lifts are subject to the "catapult effect"—if the wheels drop into a pothole or drive off a curb, the leverage at the end of the boom acts like a trebuchet, violently launching the occupant out of the basket.
→ Scissor Lifts: Harnesses are generally NOT required in scissor lifts (unless mandated by specific site rules), because the lifting mechanism is purely vertical and there is no catapult effect. The guardrails provide the primary fall protection.

Operating Rules:
→ Never use an EWP as a crane to lift materials outside the basket.
→ Never climb on the mid-rails or top rails of the basket to gain extra height. Keep both feet flat on the deck.
→ Never exit an EWP at height (climbing out of the basket onto a roof or structure) unless a specific, engineered procedure and risk assessment have been approved.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 3: PORTABLE LADDERS — THE MOST DANGEROUS EQUIPMENT
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Portable ladders are disproportionately responsible for serious fall injuries. They are designed for access and egress, not as a primary work platform.

Rules for Safe Ladder Use:
→ Industrial Rating: Only use ladders rated for Industrial use with a minimum load capacity of 120kg (150kg preferred). Never use domestic-rated ladders on a worksite.
→ The 4-to-1 Rule: For every 4 metres a straight or extension ladder goes up, the base must be 1 metre out from the wall.
→ Secure the Ladder: Extension ladders must be secured at the top (tied off) and bottom (footed or cleated) to prevent slipping.
→ Over-reach (The Belt Buckle Rule): Keep your belt buckle (navel) within the vertical stiles of the ladder. If you have to lean out past the stiles, move the ladder. Over-reaching shifts the centre of gravity and causes lateral tip-overs.
→ Step Ladders: Never stand on the top step or the top plate of an A-frame step ladder.
→ Three Points of Contact: You must maintain three points of contact (two feet and one hand, or two hands and one foot) when climbing or descending. Tools should be carried in a tool belt, or pulled up via a bucket and rope, leaving your hands free to climb.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 4: PREVENTING DROPPED OBJECTS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Working at heights doesn't just present a risk of the worker falling; it presents a lethal risk of tools and materials falling onto workers below. A 1kg spanner dropped from 20 metres carries enough kinetic energy to kill a worker wearing a hard hat.

Controls for Dropped Objects:
→ Toeboards: Must be fitted to all scaffolding and EWP baskets to prevent objects being kicked over the edge.
→ Tool Lanyards: Tools used at height near leading edges should be tethered to the worker's wrist, belt, or the structure using rated tool lanyards.
→ Exclusion Zones: A hard-barricaded exclusion zone must be established directly below any overhead work. The size of the zone must account for the deflection of falling objects bouncing off structures.`
