        title: "Module 3: Fall Arrest Systems — Harnesses, Lanyards & Anchors",
        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 3,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: `FALL ARREST SYSTEMS — COMPONENTS, INSPECTION & CLEARANCE CALCULATIONS

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: THE FULL BODY HARNESS (AS/NZS 1891.1)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When a fall arrest system (Level 4 control) is required, the worker must wear a full-body harness. A harness is designed to distribute the violent impact forces of a fall across the strongest parts of the human skeleton: the pelvis, thighs, and chest.

Fitting the Harness Correctly:
A poorly fitted harness is extremely dangerous. During a fall, a loose harness can ride up, causing severe genital injury, internal organ damage, or even allowing the worker to slip out of the harness entirely.
1. Check the D-ring: The dorsal (back) D-ring must sit squarely between the shoulder blades.
2. Chest Strap: Must sit flat across the mid-chest (sternum), not up near the throat (which causes strangulation during a fall).
3. Leg Straps: Must be snug. The "flat hand rule" applies—you should be able to slide a flat hand between the strap and your thigh, but not a closed fist.

Pre-Use Inspection:
Harnesses must be inspected by the user before every single use. Check for:
→ Webbing: Cuts, fraying, UV degradation (fading, stiffness), chemical damage, or weld splatter.
→ Stitching: Broken, pulled, or missing threads in the load-bearing patterns.
→ Hardware: Cracks, distortion, or severe corrosion on D-rings, buckles, and adjusters.
→ Fall Indicator: Most modern harnesses have folded stitching near the dorsal D-ring designed to rip open if the harness is subjected to a fall force. If the indicator is deployed, the harness MUST be immediately tagged out and destroyed.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: LANYARDS AND SHOCK ABSORBERS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

A standard fall arrest lanyard is maximum 2.0 metres in length. It must ALWAYS incorporate a personal energy absorber (shock absorber).

The Physics of Fall Arrest:
If a 100kg worker falls 2 metres attached to a static (non-stretching) rope, the sudden stop generates an impact force of over 20 KiloNewtons (kN)—more than 2 tonnes of force. This will snap the spine, rupture internal organs, and cause fatal injury.

Under Australian Standards, the maximum allowable impact force on the human body during fall arrest is 6 kN (approximately 600 kg).

The Shock Absorber:
To keep forces below 6 kN, the shock absorber is designed to tear open progressively, deploying an additional length of webbing (up to 1.75 metres) to decelerate the falling worker gradually.
→ You MUST ensure the shock absorber pack is attached to the harness D-ring end, not the anchor end, to prevent the heavy hardware from whipping back and striking you during a fall.
→ Twin-Tail Lanyards: Used for 100% tie-off when moving along a structure. You clip the second tail to the new anchor before un-clipping the first tail. NEVER clip the unused tail back onto your harness webbing or side D-rings, as this bypasses the shock absorber during a fall.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 3: ANCHOR POINTS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The strongest harness and lanyard are useless if the anchor point fails.

Anchor Load Ratings (AS/NZS 1891.4):
→ A single-person free-fall arrest anchor must be rated to withstand 15 kN (approximately 1.5 tonnes) of force.
→ For two people, the anchor must be rated to 21 kN.
→ You cannot simply tie off to a piece of scaffold tube, a PVC pipe, or a roof truss unless it has been certified by a competent person as meeting the 15 kN requirement.

The Pendulum Effect:
Always anchor as directly above your head as possible. If you anchor far to your left or right and fall, you will swing like a pendulum.
→ The pendulum swing can cause you to smash into adjacent structures with massive force.
→ The lanyard can drag across a sharp edge, sawing through the webbing and causing complete failure.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 4: FALL CLEARANCE CALCULATIONS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

A fall arrest system is completely useless if the worker hits the ground before the system fully deploys. You must calculate the required fall clearance distance before working.

The Calculation:
To determine the safe clearance distance required below the anchor point, you must add:
1. Lanyard Length: (e.g., 2.0 metres)
2. Shock Absorber Maximum Tear-Out Length: (e.g., 1.75 metres)
3. Height of the Worker: (e.g., 1.8 metres)
4. Safety Clearance Buffer: (Mandatory 1.0 metre buffer to account for harness stretch and D-ring slide).

Total Required Clearance = 2.0 + 1.75 + 1.8 + 1.0 = 6.55 Metres.

This means if your anchor point is 6.55 metres or less from the ground (or an obstruction below), a standard 2-metre shock-absorbing lanyard WILL NOT STOP YOU from hitting the ground.

Solutions for Low Clearance:
If you do not have 6.55m of clearance, you must use alternative equipment:
→ Self-Retracting Lifelines (Inertia Reels): These act like car seatbelts, locking almost instantly and arresting the fall within a few centimetres, requiring drastically less clearance.
→ Shorter Lanyards.
→ Work Positioning / Restraint techniques.`
